<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hebat</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Hepat</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hebat was a mother goddess of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hurrians">Hurrians</a>, worshipped as the consort of the storm god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a>, especially in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and Kizzuwatna. Her cult centres included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kummanni">Kummanni</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittites</a> came to identify her with the sun goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a>. She is depicted at the rock sanctuary of Yazılıkaya leading the procession of goddesses.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hebat.jpg" alt="Hebat" width="320" height="212" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bernard Gagnon · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chamber_A,_Yazilikaya_06.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Her worship had its roots in the north of modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. Texts from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> of the third millennium BCE name her Ḫalabāytu, which Alfonso Archi reads as she of Ḫalab, that is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>; another proposal derives the name from the root ḫbb, to love. She was a minor goddess at Ebla, always linked there with Aleppo, and was already paired with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadad">Adad</a>, the weather god of that city. Later she was worshipped in the kingdom of Yamhad, at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alalakh">Alalakh</a> and at Emar, where she was associated with sikkānu stones, and names such as Puduḫepa and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abdi-heba">Abdi-Heba</a>, servant of Ḫepat, invoke her.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Epithets such as queen, lady of heaven and queen of heaven marked her high place, and a Hurrian ceremony for her concerned allašši, ladyship, as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a>'s concerned kingship. She could also be invoked in rites of midwifery. In Hurrian offering lists, the kaluti, she heads a circle of deities that includes her son Šarruma, her daughters Allanzu and Kunzišalli and her attendant Takitu. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Ullikummi, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kumarbi-cycle">Kumarbi Cycle</a>, the monster blocks the entrance of her temple, cutting her off from the other gods, and she sends Takitu to learn the fate of her husband Teshub.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>She held the first place among the goddesses in the Hurrian traditions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> and Kizzuwatna, displacing Šauška, who kept that place in eastern centres such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuzi">Nuzi</a>. Hittite sources first mention her when Ḫattušili I carried her statue off from Ḫaššum, and she and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teshub">Teshub</a> later headed the dynastic pantheon. At Yazılıkaya she stands on a leopard with her children. Queen Puduḫepa's prayer equates her with the Sun goddess of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arinna">Arinna</a>, though scholars such as Piotr Taracha doubt the idea reached ordinary worship. In the first millennium BCE she was paired with the Luwian weather god Tarḫunz in Tabal, and the goddess Hipta, known from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydia">Lydia</a>, may be a late form of her.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ḫepat” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the Middle and Late Bronze Age Hurrian and Mitannian influence was strong: the Hurrian goddess <b>Hebat</b> was worshipped in Jerusalem, and Baal was held equivalent to the Hurrian storm god Teshub and the Hittite Tarhunt.</blockquote><a href="/religion/canaanite-religion">Canaanite religion · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>In an inscription she invokes the sun goddess of Arinna, queen of all lands, who in the land of Hatti has that name and in the land of cedars the name <b>Hebat</b>.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hittite-religion">Hittite religion · Legacy</a></li><li><blockquote>The texts show it as a prosperous city with temples of the storm god and his consort <b>Hebat</b>, reflecting the Hurrian and Syrian culture of the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/hassum">Hassum</a></li><li><blockquote>The population mixed Luwian and Hurrian elements, and its temples of Teshub and <b>Hebat</b> and its priests specialised in purification rituals.</blockquote><a href="/state/kizzuwatna">Kizzuwatna</a></li></ul></section>
